comments.body:
@stephanemagnenat following up on the `std::execution::par` question with code and numbers: https://github.com/Globulation2/glob2/pull/212.

Short version: there is no batch of independent fields to parallelise. Building fields are built lazily inside the unit step that first needs them, and the resource/area fields are refreshed one per tick round robin, so a parallel algorithm would have one item. What does pay is overlapping that one round-robin field with the units step: seed it from the live map at the tick start, propagate it on a worker thread while the teams step, publish it by a pointer swap exactly 3 ticks later. Fixed seed and publication ticks keep it deterministic (per-tick checksum sidecars are identical across plain runs, a run pinned to one core and a run under CPU load; and identical to a synchronous variant during development). Whole-game wall time against master: 512², 8 Nicowars: 44.1 s to 32.1 s; 128², 2 Nicowars: 3.34 s to 2.65 s. Economy unchanged within noise over 36 and 24 seeds. Details, code notes and the measurement recipe are in the PR.

@genixpro the one thing from your gradient contract comments that this changes: the propagation scratch is now an explicit `GradientScratch` per thread (main thread's in the propagation TU, the worker's on its own stack) instead of the shared static, which is what your comment asked for before any parallel use.
